<!-- Itinerary -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrapper">
    <p class="section-label">Programme</p>
    <h2 style="margin-bottom:.5rem;">Day-by-Day Itinerary</h2>
    <p style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--fg-light);margin-bottom:2rem;">The programme below reflects what actually happened. Deviations from the original plan are noted in italics.</p>

    <div class="itinerary">

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">2 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Arrival in Entebbe</strong>
          <p>The group arrived at Entebbe International Airport at 22:50 and transferred to the hotel. Stay: Best Western Premier Hotel, Entebbe.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">3 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>NaCORI Labs, Kituza Farm &amp; CCPAU Cupping</strong>
          <p>Tour of NaCORI's clone research labs and mother gardens, then Kituza Coffee Farm in Mukono. In the evening, a professional cupping session hosted by Ian Clarke (Clarke Farm Ltd / CCPAU) presented 32 lots (R1–R32) from cooperatives across the programme — the format benchmark named explicitly by multiple participants in the post-trip survey. Stay: Best Western Premier, Entebbe.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">4 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>MAAIF Ministerial Visit</strong>
          <p>Meeting with the Minister of Agriculture, Animal Industry and Fisheries at MAAIF headquarters, focused on value addition and Uganda's specialty positioning, followed by a press briefing. <em>Deviation: the planned visit to Ian Clarke's farm in Kyarusozi was dropped for time; the group travelled directly to Nyaika Hotel.</em></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">5 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Mountain Harvest Processing Plant &amp; Queen Elizabeth NP</strong>
          <p>Morning at Mountain Harvest's primary processing station in the Rwenzori mountains (wet processing, fermentation, raised-bed drying) — one of Uganda's most export-ready specialty operators. Afternoon: equator crossing at Kikolongo, Kazinga Channel boat cruise, evening game drive in Queen Elizabeth National Park. Stay: Parkview Safari Lodge.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">6 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Africa Coffee Park &amp; ACPCU Ankole Organic Producers</strong>
          <p>Morning game drive, then Africa Coffee Park in Rwashamaire (nurseries, demonstration plots, 120 kg roasting facility). <em>Deviation: the planned cattle experience at Emburara was replaced by a meeting with an Ankole Organic Producers Cooperative Union (ACPCU) member</em> — insight into organic certification and smallholder economics. Stay: Emburara Lodge.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">7 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Hope Coffee Cooperative, Sanje &amp; Masaka</strong>
          <p><em>Deviation: the planned visit to Kiruhura Farm was replaced by a morning at Hope Coffee Cooperative, Sanje</em> (Celebrate Hope Ministries) — clear lot segregation, transparent farmer pricing, and community benefit documentation relevant to EUDR traceability. Afternoon farm walk at Victor's Kabonera Farm, Masaka, then travel to Kampala. Stay: Four Points Kampala.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">8 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Little Farms Coffee, Kakooge · Jinja</strong>
          <p>Farm visit at Little Farms Coffee, Kakooge — direct-to-consumer storytelling model plus an on-site Black Soldier Fly facility turning by-products into fertiliser and feed. Afternoon travel to Jinja. Stay: Source of the Nile / Nile Village Hotel.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">9 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Sera Wild Farm, Kapchorwa — High-Altitude Arabica</strong>
          <p>Transit to Kapchorwa on the slopes of Mount Elgon. Sera Wild Farm — high-altitude Arabica (SL28, Bugisu, experimental varieties) that generated the strongest enthusiasm of the trip. Afternoon: Sipi Falls. Stay: Mount Elgon Hotel.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="itin-day">
        <div class="itin-date">10 May</div>
        <div class="itin-content">
          <strong>Final Debrief &amp; MAAIF Cupping Session</strong>
          <p>Return to Kampala for a debrief at Coffee House and a second technical cupping, hosted by MAAIF at the Uganda Coffee Development Authority lab, Lugogo. Transfer to Entebbe Airport in the evening.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Coffee Highlights -->
<section class="section section-bg">
  <div class="wrapper">
    <p class="section-label">Coffee Highlights</p>
    <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Three stations stood out as the strongest commercial reference points</h2>
    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(280px,1fr));gap:1.25rem;">
      <div class="step">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.35rem;">Mountain Harvest — Rwenzori</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--fg-mid);margin:0;">One of Uganda's most export-ready specialty operators, sourcing from smallholders on the mid- and upper slopes of the Rwenzori mountains (1,400–1,900 m). Equipped for washed and natural processing with temperature-monitored fermentation and 60+ raised drying beds. Rainforest Alliance certification available; in discussion for Brussels World of Coffee (June 2026) and the Munich Uganda Festival.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.35rem;">Hope Coffee Cooperative — Sanje</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--fg-mid);margin:0;">Operated under Celebrate Hope Ministries, integrating economic inclusion for women and youth with commercial coffee production. Strong social accountability documentation and clear farmer-group traceability — directly relevant to EUDR due diligence and story-backed sourcing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <h3 style="margin-bottom:.35rem;">Ian Clarke / CCPAU Cupping — Lugogo</h3>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem;color:var(--fg-mid);margin:0;">The most commercially actionable station of the trip: 32 lots (R1–R32) presented with variety, region, processing, altitude, certification and available volume — a producer contact attached to every cup. Named by multiple respondents as the format benchmark for future trips.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <img src="/images/uganda-trip-cupping.jpg" alt="CCPAU cupping session with Ian Clarke, Lugogo, Kampala" style="width:100%;max-height:420px;object-fit:cover;border-radius:var(--radius);display:block;">
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Regions covered -->
